all right packs rundown recap rerun cap  down so packs prime is already long over  and everyone else has covered the bigger  stuff the news the panels the highlights  the cosplay the awesome people the  parties so what's my take on it that's  relevant to me ends of the channel well  there's the human element the overall  story of flying down from Canada seeing  the west coast forest fires from the sky  the smoke gathering in the mountain  valleys as the Sun sets there's standing  in line followed by more standing in  line followed by standing in large  crowds  there's hanging out with awesome people  at parties there's meeting people you  deeply respect and turning into a  blubbering fanboy meeting Felicia really  was the high point for me though no joke  it was it was big for me  so there's all the noise and chaos and  fun and boredom and new short term  friends and all of that but what's to be  sussed out well here's something to talk  about are you familiar with pin trading  so Penny Arcade is something that the PA  people started a few years ago based off  of the pin trading thing that Disneyland  does the PA merch team along with a  handful of other companies like Wizards  of the coast make official trading pins  and then fans collect and trade them and  that right there is pretty much the line  that's the sum total volume of  information that 90% of you care about  ever hearing but bear with me for a  second so there's an internal economy to  the trading different pins are of course  harder or easier to get a hold of some  are produced in huge numbers and are  widely available at a variety of  different events while others are  produced in incredibly limited  quantities and available only through  ridiculously circuitous means some are  just sold as general myrcella's cost  time or effort or just dumb luck the  community has developed its own jargon  to describe the economy such as a pay  pin versus a play pin a pin that can be  bought as general merge versus one that  you get by playing a company's demo  fodder pins or low value pins that are  used to fuel other trades especially  with the Penny Arcade staff who have  special trading rules and their own  unique pin various people gain some  measure of status or notoriety in the  community by virtue of having a large  election or being a toxic traitor or  just being particularly active on the  community forums in fact on Sunday night  because I am nothing if not a huge nerd  I went to the big trading event at the  Westin Hotel my modest collection of  pins hanging around my neck on a lanyard  and I stumbled into subculture history  as I watched two people spend the better  part of an hour hashing out a trade over  the coveted Kickstarter pin and despite  the fact that I had never before heard  of this pin prior to the event and was  in every way shape and form a new  initiative oaken I found myself a part  of the enthralled crowd watching the  trade my excitement rising as they  bargained running down checklists and  counting and recounting the inventory at  stake and along with everyone else  watching I clapped and cheered when they  shook hands and sealed the trade it was  fascinating  it was subculture in motion a microcosm  of language and culture and value and I  got caught up in it in a big way in fact  I spent most of the convention chasing  down pins getting duplicates that I  could use to trade for pins that I  wanted making trades for pins that I  figure other people will want in the  future and that in turn will let me  leverage four pins that I want to have I  arrived on Friday with a single lanyard  of straggler pins left over from 2014  and by Monday I had upgraded to a pin  wallet because as my friend said I was  starting to look like mr. T and I  jingled when I walked and you know what  I enjoyed it because and maybe this is  just something that's been on my mind  lately  but it's easy to become cynical and to  associate criticism with cynicism you go  to something like pax and it's huge and  bombastic and loud tens of thousands of  square feet dedicated to companies  erecting miniature temples to themselves  investing tens of thousands of dollars  into getting people hyped for their  products the pin trading itself is root  and branch an alternate revenue stream  that doubles as promotional material I  spent a ridiculous amount of time around  mo t cos booth for gigantic because the  T open was a pain in the ass to get but  I wanted it both for completion and  because it's actually pretty sweet yes  it was 100% promotional and it worked I  hadn't even heard of mo t go or gigantic  prior to pax but now I'm honestly kind  of excited to play the beta when it goes  live so here's the thing just because  there's people out there whose job  is to generate hype doesn't mean we  should feel bad about being hyped we can  be both things we can be critical of  culture and its products we can be aware  of the mechanisms of subculture and how  we interface with them we can and should  be cognizant and critical and even a  little suspicious of where corporation  and culture meet where subcultures form  around consumption and we can still let  ourselves get excited and enjoy the  human connections that we form in those  spaces as microcosmic and oblique as  they may be and so go in peace my  children in the name of like share and  subscribe  amen
 

